# 1. Purpose and Scope

This specification defines all steps required to migrate the OPEX Budgeting System (OBS) from its local desktop development state to the Azure cloud environment using PostgreSQL Flexible Server as the data platform, Azure App Service as the primary API host, and Azure Functions as the batch ingestion pipeline executor. It is the canonical Azure cloud migration specification for OBS and implements the platform baseline established in Application Architecture Specification v3.6.

This document is structured to be consumed directly by Claude Code and the development team. Every step is a discrete, verifiable task with explicit acceptance criteria. Steps are sequenced in strict dependency order.

## 1.1 Architectural Decision Record

This specification constitutes the architectural decision record for the following decisions that implement the OBS Application Architecture Specification v3.6 baseline:

| **ADR** | **Architecture Spec v3.6 Baseline** | **This Specification Implementation** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ADR-PG-01 | Data platform: Azure PostgreSQL Flexible Server (Section 4.2) | Data platform: Azure PostgreSQL Flexible Server. All obs.* tables are PostgreSQL tables in the obs schema. The obs schema is the single source of truth. |
| ADR-PG-02 | Primary API host: Azure App Service, Linux, Python 3.11 (Section 2.2) | Primary API host: Azure App Service (Linux, Python 3.11), provisioned as the single API hosting track. |
| ADR-PG-03 | Batch processing: Azure Functions (Section 4.2) | Batch processing: Azure Functions (timer-triggered for actuals and employees; Blob Storage event-triggered for hierarchies). |
| ADR-PG-04 | DB_ENGINE environment variable: duckdb / postgresql | DB_ENGINE environment variable: duckdb / postgresql. The postgresql branch in engine.py is the cloud target; the duckdb branch remains the local development engine. |

| **Claude Code note — hosting-agnostic design honoured** | ***The codebase was intentionally designed to be hosting-agnostic. The DB_ENGINE switch in engine.py and the environment-variable-driven configuration layer mean this migration is an infrastructure provisioning and targeted code extension exercise — not a rewrite. Only engine.py, helpers.py, the pipeline scripts, and requirements.txt require changes.*** |
| --- | --- |

## 1.2 Migration Stage Overview

| **Stage** | **Name** | **Summary** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Azure Infrastructure Provisioning | Resource group, PostgreSQL Flexible Server, Azure Blob Storage, Key Vault, App Service Plan + Web App, Static Web Apps, Application Insights. |
| 2 | Identity and Access Configuration | Entra ID application registration; App Service managed identity; RBAC grants to PostgreSQL, Blob Storage, and Key Vault. |
| 3 | PostgreSQL Schema Bootstrap | Create the obs schema and all obs.* tables using PostgreSQL DDL; configure audit log trigger for append-only enforcement; seed the first Administrator user. |
| 4 | Code Changes — PostgreSQL Engine Branch | Add DB_ENGINE=postgresql branch to engine.py and helpers.py; add psycopg2 to requirements.txt; audit the merge() helper. |
| 5 | Application Deployment and Configuration | Deploy FastAPI to App Service; deploy React SPA to Static Web Apps; configure all environment variables and Key Vault references; activate Entra ID OIDC flow. |
| 6 | Azure Functions Ingestion Pipeline | Deploy three Azure Functions (actuals timer, employees timer, hierarchies blob trigger); configure Blob Storage bindings; verify idempotency. |
| 7 | End-to-End Validation | Full budget cycle validation across all OBS modules. |

# 2. Prerequisites

All items below must be confirmed before Stage 1 begins. An unresolved prerequisite blocks all downstream stages.

## 2.1 Access and Permissions

| **Prerequisite** | **Required Access Level** |
| --- | --- |
| Azure subscription | Contributor or Owner on the target subscription |
| Microsoft Entra ID tenant | Application Administrator or Global Administrator role |
| CI/CD pipeline | Write access to the repository and pipeline secret store |
| DNS / custom domain (optional) | DNS management access if a custom domain is required |

## 2.2 Local Environment Verified

The Phase 0 Prerequisite Gate from OBS Local Dev Environment Spec v1.0 must be passed before cloud migration begins:

- make db-bootstrap and make db-seed complete without errors against the local DuckDB instance.

- make api starts FastAPI and GET /health returns HTTP 200.

- npm run dev starts the Vite frontend and the browser at http://localhost:5173 loads the application shell.

- make test-unit passes with zero failures.

## 2.3 Codebase Readiness Checks

| **Check** | **Verification Method** |
| --- | --- |
| LOCAL_AUTH_BYPASS startup assertion is present | Review backend/app/auth/middleware.py — startup code must raise if LOCAL_AUTH_BYPASS=true and host is not localhost. |
| DB_ENGINE switch is implemented in engine.py | Confirm engine.py branches on DB_ENGINE. The postgresql branch is added in Stage 4 alongside the existing duckdb branch. |
| No hardcoded credentials in any file | Run: grep -r 'password\│secret\│token\│key' backend/ --include='*.py' │ grep -v '.env\│Key Vault\│test' |
| CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS is environment-variable-driven | Review backend/app/main.py CORS middleware — origin must come from settings, not hardcoded. |
| ALERT_CHANNEL is environment-variable-driven | Pipeline alert channel must be controlled by the ALERT_CHANNEL environment variable. |
| Frontend values use import.meta.env | Review frontend/src — VITE_API_BASE_URL and VITE_AUTH_ENABLED must be read from Vite env, not hardcoded. |

# 3. Stage 1 — Azure Infrastructure Provisioning

Provision all Azure resources. All resources must be created within the same Azure region to minimise latency and data transfer costs.

## 3.1 Resource Group

| **az group create \   --name obs-prod-rg \   --location ****<****azure-region****>** |
| --- |

Acceptance criteria: Resource group exists. All subsequent resources in this stage are deployed into it.

## 3.2 Azure PostgreSQL Flexible Server

PostgreSQL Flexible Server is the data platform. It serves as the single source of truth for all OBS data. Configure High Availability mode to meet the 99.5% monthly uptime SLA required by Architecture Spec v3.6 Section 4.4.

| **az postgres flexible-server create \   --name obs-prod-pg \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --location ****<****azure-region****>**** \   --admin-user obsadmin \   --admin-password ****<****strong-password-stored-in-keyvault****>**** \   --sku-name Standard_D2s_v3 \   --tier GeneralPurpose \   --storage-size 128 \   --version 16 \   --high-availability ZoneRedundant \   --backup-retention 30 \   --geo-redundant-backup Enabled** |
| --- |

After creation, create the OBS application database and the obs schema:

| **# Connect to the server (use Azure Cloud Shell or psql with SSL) psql ****"****host=obs-prod-pg.postgres.database.azure.com \       port=5432 dbname=postgres \       user=obsadmin sslmode=require****"**** -- Create the application database CREATE DATABASE obs_db; -- Connect to obs_db and create the obs schema \c obs_db CREATE SCHEMA obs;** |
| --- |

Store the connection string in Key Vault:

| **az keyvault secret set \   --vault-name obs-prod-kv \   --name obs-pg-connection-string \   --value ****"****host=obs-prod-pg.postgres.database.azure.com port=5432 dbname=obs_db \            user=obsadmin password=****<****password****>**** sslmode=require****"** |
| --- |

Acceptance criteria:

- PostgreSQL Flexible Server is provisioned with Zone-Redundant HA.

- obs_db database and obs schema exist.

- Connection string is stored in Key Vault as obs-pg-connection-string.

- The admin password is not stored in any file or CI/CD pipeline definition — Key Vault only.

- SSL is required for all connections (sslmode=require enforced).

| **Claude Code note — PostgreSQL vs DuckDB type compatibility** | ***PostgreSQL and DuckDB share nearly identical SQL syntax for the obs.***** schemas. Key differences to handle in the PostgreSQL DDL: (1) Use TEXT instead of STRING for all string columns. (2) Use NUMERIC(15,2) instead of DECIMAL(15,2) for monetary amounts — both are valid in PostgreSQL but NUMERIC is conventional. (3) Use NUMERIC(6,4) for percentage rates. (4) Use UUID type for primary keys — PostgreSQL has a native UUID type unlike DuckDB. (5) Use TIMESTAMPTZ (timestamp with time zone) instead of TIMESTAMP, storing all values in UTC. (6) The obs.audit_log append-only constraint is enforced via a PostgreSQL trigger (see Stage 3) rather than a storage-layer property.*** |
| --- | --- |

## 3.3 Azure Blob Storage Account

Create the storage account for the file landing zone. Blob Storage is the integration landing zone for all source-system files. Configure Zone-Redundant Storage (ZRS) per Architecture Spec v3.6 Section 4.4.

| **az storage account create \   --name obsprodlanding \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --sku Standard_ZRS \   --kind StorageV2 # Create required containers az storage container create --name landing-actuals      --account-name obsprodlanding az storage container create --name landing-employees    --account-name obsprodlanding az storage container create --name landing-hierarchies  --account-name obsprodlanding az storage container create --name archive              --account-name obsprodlanding az storage container create --name error                --account-name obsprodlanding** |
| --- |

Store the storage connection string in Key Vault:

| **az keyvault secret set \   --vault-name obs-prod-kv \   --name obs-blob-storage-connection-string \   --value ****"****$(az storage account show-connection-string \     --name obsprodlanding --resource-group obs-prod-rg --query connectionString -o tsv)****"** |
| --- |

Acceptance criteria:

- Storage account is ZRS-replicated.

- All five containers exist: landing-actuals, landing-employees, landing-hierarchies, archive, error.

- All containers are private — no anonymous access.

- Connection string is stored in Key Vault as obs-blob-storage-connection-string.

## 3.4 Azure Key Vault

| **az keyvault create \   --name obs-prod-kv \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --enable-soft-delete true \   --retention-days 90** |
| --- |

Required secrets to be populated across all stages:

| **Secret Name** | **Purpose** |
| --- | --- |
| obs-pg-connection-string | PostgreSQL Flexible Server connection string (see Section 3.2) |
| obs-blob-storage-connection-string | Blob Storage account connection string (see Section 3.3) |
| obs-entra-tenant-id | Microsoft Entra ID tenant ID for OIDC authentication |
| obs-entra-client-id | OBS application client ID registered in Entra ID |
| obs-cors-allowed-origins | Comma-separated list of allowed frontend origins |
| obs-appinsights-connection-string | Application Insights connection string (see Section 3.6) |

## 3.5 Azure App Service

Create the App Service Plan and Web App that will host the FastAPI backend. App Service (Linux, Python 3.11) is the single API hosting track established in Architecture Spec v3.6 Section 2.2.

| **az appservice plan create \   --name obs-prod-asp \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --sku P2v3 \   --is-linux \   --number-of-workers 2 az webapp create \   --name obs-prod-api \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --plan obs-prod-asp \   --runtime PYTHON:3.11** |
| --- |

Enable the system-assigned managed identity on the Web App — this is used for Key Vault access:

| **az webapp identity assign \   --name obs-prod-api \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg** |
| --- |

Note the principal ID returned — it is used in Stage 2 to grant Key Vault access.

Configure the startup command so App Service uses Gunicorn with Uvicorn workers:

| **az webapp config set \   --name obs-prod-api \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --startup-file ****"****gunicorn -w 4 -k uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker backend.app.main:app****"** |
| --- |

Acceptance criteria:

- App Service Plan is P2v3 Linux with 2 workers minimum.

- Web App is provisioned with Python 3.11 runtime.

- System-assigned managed identity is enabled — principal ID is noted for Stage 2.

- Startup command is set to Gunicorn + UvicornWorker.

## 3.6 Azure Static Web Apps

| **az staticwebapp create \   --name obs-prod-swa \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --sku Standard \   --location ****<****region****>** |
| --- |

Acceptance criteria: Static Web App is provisioned in the Standard SKU. Default hostname is accessible over HTTPS.

## 3.7 Azure Functions App (Ingestion Pipeline)

Create the Azure Functions App that will host the three ingestion pipeline functions. A Consumption plan is appropriate for this workload — ingestion runs are short-lived, bursty, and infrequent (hourly, daily, and ad hoc).

| **# Storage account for Functions runtime state (separate from landing zone) az storage account create \   --name obsprodfnstore \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --sku Standard_LRS \   --kind StorageV2 # Functions App (Python 3.11, Linux, Consumption plan) az functionapp create \   --name obs-prod-pipeline \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --consumption-plan-location ****<****region****>**** \   --runtime python \   --runtime-version 3.11 \   --functions-version 4 \   --os-type linux \   --storage-account obsprodfnstore** |
| --- |

Enable the system-assigned managed identity on the Functions App:

| **az functionapp identity assign \   --name obs-prod-pipeline \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg** |
| --- |

Acceptance criteria:

- Functions App is provisioned on a Consumption plan with Python 3.11 runtime.

- System-assigned managed identity is enabled — principal ID noted for Stage 2.

- A separate storage account (obsprodfnstore) is used for Functions runtime state, distinct from the landing zone storage account.

## 3.8 Azure Monitor and Application Insights

| **az monitor app-insights component create \   --app obs-prod-insights \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --location ****<****region****>**** \   --kind web az keyvault secret set \   --vault-name obs-prod-kv \   --name obs-appinsights-connection-string \   --value ****"****$(az monitor app-insights component show \     --app obs-prod-insights --resource-group obs-prod-rg \     --query connectionString -o tsv)****"** |
| --- |

Acceptance criteria: Application Insights is provisioned. Connection string is stored in Key Vault as obs-appinsights-connection-string.

# 4. Stage 2 — Identity and Access Configuration

Configure Microsoft Entra ID for OIDC/OAuth 2.0 authentication and grant the App Service and Functions managed identities access to Key Vault and Blob Storage.

## 4.1 Entra ID Application Registration

Register the OBS as an OIDC Relying Party in Microsoft Entra ID:

| **Property** | **Value** |
| --- | --- |
| Application name | OPEX Budgeting System (OBS) |
| Supported account types | Single tenant — accounts in this organizational directory only |
| Platform | Single-page application (SPA) |
| Redirect URIs | https://<static-web-apps-hostname>/auth/callback and http://localhost:5173/auth/callback |
| Logout URL | https://<static-web-apps-hostname>/auth/logout |
| Token configuration | Enable ID tokens; add optional claims: email, name |
| API permissions | Microsoft Graph — User.Read (delegated) |
| Client secret | None — SPA uses PKCE flow, no client secret required |

| **az keyvault secret set --vault-name obs-prod-kv --name obs-entra-client-id --value ****"****<****client-id****>****"**** az keyvault secret set --vault-name obs-prod-kv --name obs-entra-tenant-id --value ****"****<****tenant-id****>****"** |
| --- |

## 4.2 Key Vault Access — App Service Managed Identity

Grant the App Service managed identity permission to read secrets from Key Vault:

| **az keyvault set-policy \   --name obs-prod-kv \   --object-id ****<****app-service-principal-id****>**** \   --secret-permissions get list** |
| --- |

## 4.3 Key Vault Access — Functions App Managed Identity

Grant the Functions App managed identity permission to read secrets from Key Vault:

| **az keyvault set-policy \   --name obs-prod-kv \   --object-id ****<****functions-principal-id****>**** \   --secret-permissions get list** |
| --- |

## 4.4 Blob Storage Access — Functions App Managed Identity

Grant the Functions App managed identity the Storage Blob Data Contributor role on the landing zone storage account:

| **az role assignment create \   --assignee ****<****functions-principal-id****>**** \   --role ****"****Storage Blob Data Contributor****"**** \   --scope /subscriptions/****<****sub-id****>****/resourceGroups/obs-prod-rg/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/obsprodlanding** |
| --- |

## 4.5 PostgreSQL Access — App Service and Functions

PostgreSQL Flexible Server uses password authentication. The connection string (including password) is stored in Key Vault and read by the App Service and Functions App at startup via their managed identities. No static credentials appear in code or configuration files.

Additionally, configure the PostgreSQL firewall to allow connections from the App Service and Functions App outbound IP addresses:

| **# Retrieve outbound IPs for App Service az webapp show --name obs-prod-api --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --query outboundIpAddresses -o tsv # Add each outbound IP as a firewall rule az postgres flexible-server firewall-rule create \   --name obs-prod-pg \   --resource-group obs-prod-rg \   --rule-name allow-app-service-1 \   --start-ip-address ****<****ip****>**** \   --end-ip-address ****<****ip****>** |
| --- |

| **Claude Code note — PostgreSQL firewall and Azure Functions** | ***Azure Functions on a Consumption plan uses dynamic outbound IP addresses that can change. For a more robust configuration, consider deploying the Functions App into a Virtual Network (VNet Integration) and allowing the entire VNet subnet. Alternatively, enable the ******'******Allow access to Azure services******'****** option on the PostgreSQL firewall during initial setup and restrict it via VNet integration before going to production.*** |
| --- | --- |

## 4.6 CI/CD Service Principal

| **az ad sp create-for-rbac \   --name obs-cicd-sp \   --role Contributor \   --scopes /subscriptions/****<****sub-id****>****/resourceGroups/obs-prod-rg \   --sdk-auth** |
| --- |

Store the output JSON as a CI/CD pipeline secret. Never commit to the repository.

Stage 2 acceptance criteria:

- Entra ID application registration exists with correct redirect URIs. Client ID and tenant ID are in Key Vault.

- App Service managed identity has get and list permissions on Key Vault.

- Functions App managed identity has get and list permissions on Key Vault and Storage Blob Data Contributor on the landing zone.

- No static credentials appear in any source file or CI/CD definition.

# 5. Stage 3 — PostgreSQL Schema Bootstrap

Create all obs.* tables in the PostgreSQL database. The existing schema/bootstrap.sql was written for DuckDB. A new file — schema/bootstrap_pg.sql — must be authored for PostgreSQL. It is idempotent throughout using CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS.

## 5.1 PostgreSQL DDL Conversion Rules

Apply the following translation rules when authoring schema/bootstrap_pg.sql from the DuckDB schema:

| **DuckDB Type** | **PostgreSQL Type** | **Notes** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| STRING NOT NULL | TEXT NOT NULL | TEXT is the idiomatic PostgreSQL string type |
| STRING (nullable) | TEXT | Omit NOT NULL constraint |
| DECIMAL(15,2) | NUMERIC(15,2) | Monetary amounts |
| DECIMAL(6,4) | NUMERIC(6,4) | Percentage rates (e.g., 0.0765) |
| BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE | BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE | Identical syntax |
| TIMESTAMP | TIMESTAMPTZ | Always store in UTC; TIMESTAMPTZ enforces timezone awareness |
| STRING (UUID primary key) | UUID NOT NULL DEFAULT gen_random_uuid() | PostgreSQL native UUID; auto-generation via gen_random_uuid() removes the need for application-side UUID generation on INSERT |
| Z-ORDER BY hints | Not applicable | Z-ORDER is a Delta Lake concept. Use PostgreSQL indexes instead. See Section 5.2. |
| TBLPROPERTIES (delta.appendOnly) | PostgreSQL trigger | Append-only enforcement is implemented via a BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE trigger on obs.audit_log. See Section 5.3. |

## 5.2 Index Strategy

Replace Delta Lake Z-ORDER hints with PostgreSQL indexes. The following indexes are the minimum required for OBS query performance. They must be included in schema/bootstrap_pg.sql:

| **Table** | **Required Indexes** |
| --- | --- |
| obs.actuals | (cost_center_id, fiscal_year, fiscal_month) — primary filter for reporting; (is_deleted) where is_deleted = FALSE — partial index for current-version queries |
| obs.budget_lines | (version_id, cost_center_id) — primary filter for budget planning views |
| obs.notifications | (user_id, is_read, created_at DESC) — primary filter for notification inbox queries |
| obs.audit_log | (entity_type, entity_id) — for entity history lookups; (user_id, event_timestamp DESC) — for user activity queries |
| obs.positions | (cost_center_id, fiscal_year) — primary filter for workforce planning views |
| obs.ingestion_control | (file_name, content_hash) — deduplication key lookup on every pipeline run |
| obs.users | (entra_oid) — login resolution; (is_active) WHERE is_active = TRUE — partial index for active user lookups |

## 5.3 Audit Log Append-Only Enforcement

Delta Lake enforced append-only via the delta.appendOnly table property. PostgreSQL enforces it via a trigger. Include the following in schema/bootstrap_pg.sql:

| **-- Trigger function: raise an error on any UPDATE or DELETE against obs.audit_log CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION obs.audit_log_immutable() RETURNS TRIGGER AS $$ BEGIN   RAISE EXCEPTION ****'****obs.audit_log is append-only. UPDATE and DELETE are not permitted.****'****; END; $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql; -- Apply the trigger to obs.audit_log CREATE TRIGGER enforce_audit_log_immutable BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE ON obs.audit_log FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION obs.audit_log_immutable();** |
| --- |

| **Claude Code note — audit log enforcement parity** | ***The application-layer enforcement (never calling UPDATE or DELETE on obs.audit_log) remains in place as required by the Claude Code Implementation Guide Rule 5. The PostgreSQL BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE trigger adds a second enforcement layer at the database level. Both layers are required.*** |
| --- | --- |

## 5.4 Bootstrap Execution

| **# From the repository root, with psql installed psql ****"****$(az keyvault secret show --vault-name obs-prod-kv \   --name obs-pg-connection-string --query value -o tsv)****"**** \   -f schema/bootstrap_pg.sql** |
| --- |

The bootstrap is idempotent. All CREATE TABLE, CREATE INDEX, and CREATE TRIGGER statements use IF NOT EXISTS (or OR REPLACE for functions). It is safe to re-run after a partial failure.

## 5.5 Schema Bootstrap Acceptance Criteria

| **Criterion** | **Verification** |
| --- | --- |
| All obs.* tables exist | SELECT table_name FROM information_schema.tables WHERE table_schema = 'obs' returns all expected table names. |
| obs.audit_log trigger is active | SELECT trigger_name FROM information_schema.triggers WHERE event_object_table = 'audit_log' returns enforce_audit_log_immutable. |
| Append-only is enforced | Execute: UPDATE obs.audit_log SET outcome = 'FAILURE' WHERE 1=0; Expect: ERROR — obs.audit_log is append-only. |
| All indexes are created | SELECT indexname FROM pg_indexes WHERE schemaname = 'obs' lists all required indexes from Section 5.2. |
| UUID auto-generation works | INSERT INTO obs.users (user_id, ...) VALUES (gen_random_uuid(), ...) succeeds without application-side UUID generation. |

## 5.6 Production Data Seeding

| **Data** | **Action Required** |
| --- | --- |
| First Administrator user | INSERT one record into obs.users with is_administrator = TRUE and is_active = TRUE using the Entra ID oid of the designated system administrator. |
| Fiscal calendar | Insert the current fiscal year definition. The system requires at least one active fiscal year to be operational. |
| Cost center hierarchy | Import via the Administrator landing zone upload after deployment. |
| Account hierarchy | Import via the Administrator landing zone upload after deployment. |
| Business rules | Enter via the Administration module Business Rules Configuration screen after deployment. |

| **Claude Code note — do not run dev seed scripts against production** | ***The schema/seed/ scripts (01_users.sql through 07_actuals.sql) contain synthetic DuckDB development data. They must never be executed against the production PostgreSQL database. The DuckDB seed data also uses DuckDB-specific syntax that is not valid PostgreSQL SQL.*** |
| --- | --- |

# 6. Stage 4 — Code Changes — PostgreSQL Engine Branch

This is the only stage that requires changes to the application codebase. All changes are confined to the database access layer. No changes are required to routers, services, Pydantic models, or frontend code.

| **Claude Code note — scope of code changes** | ***The hosting-agnostic design means the API layer, calculation service, auth middleware, and all business logic are entirely unaffected. The four files that require changes are: backend/requirements.txt (add psycopg2-binary), backend/app/db/engine.py (add postgresql branch), backend/app/db/helpers.py (add _pg_upsert for the merge() function), and schema/bootstrap_pg.sql (new file authored in Stage 3). The pipeline scripts are replaced in Stage 6.*** |
| --- | --- |

## 6.1 requirements.txt — Add psycopg2

Add psycopg2-binary to backend/requirements.txt. This is the standard PostgreSQL adapter for Python. The binary distribution is used to avoid requiring a local libpq compilation step:

| **# backend/requirements.txt — additions only psycopg2-binary****>****=2.9** |
| --- |

Remove the following packages from requirements.txt — they are not used by the PostgreSQL data platform:

| **# Remove these lines from backend/requirements.txt databricks-sql-connector   # superseded by psycopg2-binary delta-spark                # superseded by PostgreSQL INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE** |
| --- |

| **Claude Code note — duckdb package is retained** | ***The duckdb package must remain in requirements.txt. The local development environment continues to use DB_ENGINE=duckdb. Removing duckdb from requirements.txt would break the local development workflow.*** |
| --- | --- |

## 6.2 engine.py — Add postgresql Branch

Add the postgresql branch to get_connection() in backend/app/db/engine.py. The existing duckdb branch is unchanged. DB_ENGINE legal values are duckdb and postgresql.

| **# backend/app/db/engine.py — full replacement import contextlib from app.config import settings @contextlib.contextmanager def get_connection():     ****"****"****"****     Return a database connection for the active DB_ENGINE.     Supported values:       duckdb      — local development (DuckDB file)       postgresql  — production / staging (Azure PostgreSQL Flexible Server)     ****"****"****"****     if settings.DB_ENGINE == ****'****duckdb****'****:         import duckdb         conn = duckdb.connect(settings.DUCKDB_PATH)         try:             yield conn         finally:             conn.close()     elif settings.DB_ENGINE == ****'****postgresql****'****:         import psycopg2         import psycopg2.extras  # for RealDictCursor         conn = psycopg2.connect(             settings.PG_CONNECTION_STRING,             cursor_factory=psycopg2.extras.RealDictCursor         )         conn.autocommit = False  # explicit transaction management         try:             yield conn             conn.commit()         except Exception:             conn.rollback()             raise         finally:             conn.close()     else:         raise ValueError(f****"****Unknown DB_ENGINE: {settings.DB_ENGINE!r}. ****"****                          ****"****Must be one of: duckdb, postgresql****"****)** |
| --- |

| **Claude Code note — RealDictCursor** | ***psycopg2.extras.RealDictCursor returns query results as dictionaries keyed by column name, matching the behaviour of DuckDB******'******s fetchdf() result access pattern. This ensures that all existing query code — which accesses result columns by name — works identically against both engines without modification.*** |
| --- | --- |

## 6.3 config.py — Add PG_CONNECTION_STRING Setting

Add PG_CONNECTION_STRING to the Settings class in backend/app/config.py. This follows the same pattern as DUCKDB_PATH:

| **# backend/app/config.py — additions to the Settings class class Settings(BaseSettings):     # ... existing fields ...     # Database engine selection     DB_ENGINE: str = ****"****duckdb****"****  # duckdb │ postgresql     # DuckDB (local development only)     DUCKDB_PATH: str = ****"****./local-data/obs.duckdb****"****     # PostgreSQL (production / staging)     # Value is read from Azure Key Vault via environment variable injection.     # Format: ****"****host=****<****host****>**** port=5432 dbname=obs_db user=****<****user****>**** password=****<****pw****>**** sslmode=require****"****     PG_CONNECTION_STRING: str = ****"****"****     class Config:         env_file = ****"****.env.local****"****         env_file_encoding = ****"****utf-8****"** |
| --- |

## 6.4 helpers.py — Add _pg_upsert for merge()

The merge() helper in backend/app/db/helpers.py currently branches on duckdb. Add a postgresql branch. PostgreSQL supports native INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE, which provides true atomic upsert:

| **# backend/app/db/helpers.py — merge() function replacement def merge(conn, table: str, records: list, key_cols: list):     ****"****"****"****     Idempotent upsert across all supported DB_ENGINE values.     duckdb:      DELETE + INSERT (DuckDB has no native upsert)     postgresql:  INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE (atomic upsert)     ****"****"****"****     from app.config import settings     if not records:         return     if settings.DB_ENGINE == ****'****duckdb****'****:         _duckdb_upsert(conn, table, records, key_cols)     elif settings.DB_ENGINE == ****'****postgresql****'****:         _pg_upsert(conn, table, records, key_cols)     else:         raise RuntimeError(f****"****merge() not supported for DB_ENGINE={settings.DB_ENGINE!r}****"****) def _pg_upsert(conn, table: str, records: list, key_cols: list):     ****"****"****"****     PostgreSQL INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (key_cols) DO UPDATE SET ...     This is a true atomic upsert — no intermediate DELETE step.     ****"****"****"****     if not records:         return     cols      = list(records[0].keys())     update_cols = [c for c in cols if c not in key_cols]     col_names    = ****'****, ****'****.join(cols)     placeholders = ****'****, ****'****.join(f****'****%({c})s****'**** for c in cols)     conflict_target = ****'****, ****'****.join(key_cols)     update_clause = ****'****, ****'****.join(f****'****{c} = EXCLUDED.{c}****'**** for c in update_cols)     sql = f****"****"****"****         INSERT INTO {table} ({col_names})         VALUES ({placeholders})         ON CONFLICT ({conflict_target})         DO UPDATE SET {update_clause}     ****"****"****"****     with conn.cursor() as cur:         cur.executemany(sql, records) # _duckdb_upsert() — unchanged, retained below def _duckdb_upsert(conn, table: str, records: list, key_cols: list):     if not records:         return     where = ****'**** AND ****'****.join(f****'****{k} = ?****'**** for k in key_cols)     for record in records:         key_values = [record[k] for k in key_cols]         conn.execute(f****'****DELETE FROM {table} WHERE {where}****'****, key_values)     cols = list(records[0].keys())     placeholders = ****'****, ****'****.join(****'****?****'**** * len(cols))     col_names = ****'****, ****'****.join(cols)     conn.executemany(         f****'****INSERT INTO {table} ({col_names}) VALUES ({placeholders})****'****,         [[r[c] for c in cols] for r in records]     )** |
| --- |

## 6.5 audit_write() — PostgreSQL Compatibility

The audit_write() helper in backend/app/db/helpers.py uses positional ? placeholders (DuckDB syntax). PostgreSQL uses %s positional placeholders or %(name)s named placeholders. Update audit_write() to use named placeholders that work with psycopg2:

| **# backend/app/db/helpers.py — audit_write() replacement import uuid import datetime import json def audit_write(conn, event_type: str, user_id: str, entity_type: str,                 entity_id: str, previous_value=None, new_value=None, **kwargs):     ****"****"****"****     Write an immutable record to obs.audit_log.     obs.audit_log is append-only — never call UPDATE or DELETE on it.     If this write fails, the calling transaction must roll back.     ****"****"****"****     from app.config import settings     record = {         ****'****event_id****'****:       str(uuid.uuid4()),         ****'****event_timestamp****'****: datetime.datetime.utcnow(),         ****'****event_type****'****:     event_type,         ****'****user_id****'****:        user_id,         ****'****session_id****'****:     kwargs.get(****'****session_id****'****),         ****'****entity_type****'****:    entity_type,         ****'****entity_id****'****:      entity_id,         ****'****previous_value****'****: json.dumps(previous_value) if previous_value is not None else None,         ****'****new_value****'****:      json.dumps(new_value) if new_value is not None else None,         ****'****ip_address****'****:     kwargs.get(****'****ip_address****'****),         ****'****outcome****'****:        ****'****SUCCESS****'****,     }     if settings.DB_ENGINE == ****'****duckdb****'****:         conn.execute(             ****'****INSERT INTO obs.audit_log VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)****'****,             list(record.values())         )     elif settings.DB_ENGINE == ****'****postgresql****'****:         sql = ****"****"****"****             INSERT INTO obs.audit_log               (event_id, event_timestamp, event_type, user_id, session_id,                entity_type, entity_id, previous_value, new_value, ip_address, outcome)             VALUES               (%(event_id)s, %(event_timestamp)s, %(event_type)s, %(user_id)s,                %(session_id)s, %(entity_type)s, %(entity_id)s,                %(previous_value)s, %(new_value)s, %(ip_address)s, %(outcome)s)         ****"****"****"****         with conn.cursor() as cur:             cur.execute(sql, record)** |
| --- |

## 6.6 Code Change Acceptance Criteria

Before deploying to App Service, verify all code changes locally against DuckDB to confirm nothing is broken:

- make test-unit passes with zero failures after all code changes are applied.

- DB_ENGINE=duckdb make api — the server starts without import errors. GET /health returns HTTP 200.

- DB_ENGINE=postgresql with a local PostgreSQL instance (e.g., via Docker) — the server starts, connects, and GET /health returns HTTP 200.

- get_connection() raises ValueError for any DB_ENGINE value other than duckdb or postgresql — confirm via a targeted unit test.

- No ? placeholders remain in any SQL executed via psycopg2 — psycopg2 uses %s or %(name)s only.

| **Claude Code note — SQL placeholder audit** | ***Before deploying, run a search across all backend SQL strings to confirm no DuckDB-style ? placeholders are used in code paths that execute against PostgreSQL. The correct approach is to use named placeholders %(name)s throughout, which also work with DuckDB via the parameter dict syntax. Consider migrating all SQL to use named placeholders in a single pass to eliminate the divergence entirely.*** |
| --- | --- |

